WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:14.596
story begins a long time ago when there were many gods and goddesses, and when there were many

2
00:00:14.656 --> 00:00:16.077
great warriors and mighty heroes.

3
00:00:16.697 --> 00:00:20.720
At this time many epic battles and great wars were fought because the fortune and pride of both

4
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.321
gods and men were bound together.

5
00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:27.785
Like the men that the gods and goddesses ruled, these gods and goddesses were imperfect and motivated by

6
00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:29.066
petty jealousies and fears.

7
00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:35.026
he gods and goddesses, however, rarely fought each other directly; because they were immortals, they couldn't harm each

8
00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:37.808
other. Instead they fought their battles and wars through men.

9
00:00:38.408 --> 00:00:41.931
Many warriors left to fight in these wars, but only a few returned.

10
00:00:42.591 --> 00:00:45.914
Thus the warrior's greatest dream was to return home alive and victorious.

11
00:00:46.394 --> 00:00:48.135
This was Odysseus' dream as well.

12
00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:51.258
Odysseus' dream came true.

13
00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:56.982
His resourcefulness and cunning were the foundation of his success, but most importantly it was the favor of

14
00:00:57.042 --> 00:00:59.804
Athena that truly made him seem like a god among men.

15
00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:03.365
He is perhaps best remembered as the creator of the Trojan horse.

16
00:01:04.046 --> 00:01:06.307
The plan came to him in a dream inspired by Athena.

17
00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:10.709
Athena was the goddess of wisdom and, sure enough, the plan proved to be brilliant.

18
00:01:11.349 --> 00:01:15.251
The wooden horse allowed the Greeks to trick the Trojans, because it looked like a gift when it

19
00:01:15.311 --> 00:01:16.671
was in fact a secret weapon.

20
00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:19.733
Inside the horse many Greek warriors hid and waited.

21
00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:25.215
After the Trojans pulled the horse into their city, the Greek warriors waited until nightfall and then they

22
00:01:25.355 --> 00:01:26.796
quietly crept out of the horse.

23
00:01:27.568 --> 00:01:30.991
Once they were within the mighty walls of Troy, they easily defeated the Trojans.

24
00:01:32.612 --> 00:01:36.915
After the Greeks had sacked and burned the great city of Troy, they took from its storehouses great

25
00:01:36.975 --> 00:01:39.257
quantities of gold, silver and priceless gems.

26
00:01:39.637 --> 00:01:45.041
On the shore near the still smoldering city, the Greeks erected two great pyres—one for the dead and

27
00:01:45.081 --> 00:01:46.042
the other for the gods.

28
00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:51.346
Before the Greeks left for home they first honored their dead and then they sacrificed a hundred rams

29
00:01:51.386 --> 00:01:54.128
and a hundred bulls to the gods to thank them for their triumph.

30
00:01:54.592 --> 00:01:59.415
The generous sacrifice pleased the gods, and the next morning the gods sent the Greeks a fair, easterly

31
00:01:59.455 --> 00:02:01.276
wind—a wind that would take them home.

32
00:02:01.736 --> 00:02:05.478
The Greeks rejoiced at this easterly wind for the gods had given them a priceless gift.

33
00:02:06.378 --> 00:02:10.340
Soon a thousand Greeks ships filled with the surviving heroes of the Trojan War set sail.

34
00:02:11.081 --> 00:02:15.963
Odysseus, like the others, was happy to be going home because the Trojan War had lasted for ten

35
00:02:16.003 --> 00:02:16.604
long years.

36
00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:21.126
With thoughts of his wife and son clearly in his mind, he shouted to his men, "The journey

37
00:02:21.166 --> 00:02:24.228
home for triumphant heroes ought to be pleasant, short, and sweet!"

38
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.902
His men cheered and put their backs into their oars, but none of them were aware that instead,

39
00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:31.963
their story would be one of sorrow, suffering and despair.

40
00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:34.664
For Odysseus' fate was also their fate.

41
00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:39.106
Their journey was going to be long and they were going to suffer much, because some of the

42
00:02:39.146 --> 00:02:43.608
gods disfavored Odysseus—especially the mighty god of the sea, Poseidon.

43
00:02:44.449 --> 00:02:47.950
For several days Odysseus and his companions sailed smoothly on calm seas.

44
00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.912
The men were sure that the gods favored them and that they could do no wrong.

45
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:57.542
Consequently when they came within sight of a city that belonged to their enemy, the Ciconians, Odysseus' men

46
00:02:57.642 --> 00:02:58.522
begged him to attack.

47
00:02:58.542 --> 00:03:01.564
"The gods favor us, Captain," said the men.

48
00:03:01.584 --> 00:03:06.906
"They have given us a fair wind, they have brought us to these uncivilized shores, and it would

49
00:03:06.926 --> 00:03:09.607
be a dishonor to the gods not to sack this land."

50
00:03:10.447 --> 00:03:14.749
Odysseus listened to his men and he let them persuade him that the gods had brought them here

51
00:03:14.809 --> 00:03:18.230
so that they could add more gold and silver to the treasure that was already stored in their

52
00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:23.360
holds. They attacked the city, and they easily defeated the Ciconians.

53
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.583
That night after they had plundered the city, Odysseus urged his men to return to their ships, but

54
00:03:27.623 --> 00:03:28.423
the men refused.

55
00:03:29.103 --> 00:03:32.986
They wanted to celebrate their victory, so they drank a great deal of wine and feasted on a

56
00:03:33.026 --> 00:03:36.147
great number of sheep and oxen without thanking the gods for their victory.

57
00:03:36.768 --> 00:03:41.610
The greed and disrespect of Odysseus' men did not please the gods, and the gods sent Hermes, their

58
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.412
messenger, to warn the other Ciconian tribes about the attack.

59
00:03:45.052 --> 00:03:47.934
The following morning, out of the mist, came a great army of men.

60
00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:52.729
Hermes had traveled faster than an arrow from one city to the next, and he had warned all

61
00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:54.370
the neighboring Ciconian tribes.

62
00:03:55.131 --> 00:03:58.074
Ares, the god of war, inspired these men to fight better.

63
00:03:58.694 --> 00:04:03.419
Their ability surprised Odysseus and his men, so that when they attacked they easily drove Odysseus and his

64
00:04:03.479 --> 00:04:04.460
men back to their ships.

65
00:04:05.581 --> 00:04:10.645
Once aboard their ships Odysseus and his men immediately set sail, but the angry gods sent an unforgiving

66
00:04:10.725 --> 00:04:12.467
wind to blow them into unknown waters.

67
00:04:13.108 --> 00:04:16.851
There was also sorrow in their hearts because they had lost six men from each of the twelve

68
00:04:16.891 --> 00:04:17.732
ships during the battle.

69
00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:22.353
The wind blew like a hurricane and the earth and sky were hidden from view for nine straight

70
00:04:22.393 --> 00:04:25.974
days. On the tenth day the skies miraculously cleared.

71
00:04:26.554 --> 00:04:31.015
On the horizon they spotted an island, and everyone rejoiced because their food and water were running low.

72
00:04:31.756 --> 00:04:34.836
On the island Odysseus and his men ate a simple lunch of bread and cheese.

73
00:04:35.457 --> 00:04:40.578
Odysseus then picked two men and said, "Go and find water, and if there are any inhabitants on

74
00:04:40.618 --> 00:04:42.959
this fair island see if we can buy supplies."

75
00:04:43.779 --> 00:04:45.699
The two men nodded and they set off at once.

76
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.061
As evening approached the men still had not returned.

77
00:04:49.081 --> 00:04:52.343
"I will go and find our friends," said Odysseus.

78
00:04:52.363 --> 00:04:54.964
"While I am gone, prepare the ships to sail.

79
00:04:55.564 --> 00:04:56.905
We may have to flee this island."

80
00:04:58.426 --> 00:05:02.368
Odysseus had not gone far when he met an old man who smiled peacefully at him.

81
00:05:02.388 --> 00:05:07.591
"Kind sir," began Odysseus, "have you seen two strangers traveling this road?"

82
00:05:07.611 --> 00:05:12.314
"No," replied the old man, "but why worry about them?

83
00:05:12.694 --> 00:05:16.576
They will come to no harm here in the Land of the Lotus Eaters.

84
00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:17.857
Would you like to eat this flower?

85
00:05:18.218 --> 00:05:20.139
It will protect and keep you healthy."

86
00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:24.763
Odysseus was about to eat the flowers out of respect for the old man when suddenly an owl

87
00:05:24.803 --> 00:05:25.704
screeched in alarm.

88
00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:29.167
"Athena is warning me," thought Odysseus to himself.

89
00:05:29.187 --> 00:05:31.428
"These flowers are not what they seem."

90
00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:36.693
Not long after this, Odysseus found his men, and they were happily eating the lotus flowers.

91
00:05:36.713 --> 00:05:39.415
"Let's go," said Odysseus to his men.

92
00:05:39.435 --> 00:05:41.356
"Go where?"

93
00:05:41.957 --> 00:05:42.697
they answered together.

94
00:05:42.718 --> 00:05:45.520
"Home," said Odysseus enthusiastically.

95
00:05:46.344 --> 00:05:48.506
"This is our home," they answered happily.

96
00:05:49.086 --> 00:05:52.589
Odysseus told his men to gather some lotus flowers and to follow him back to his ship.

97
00:05:52.609 --> 00:05:56.251
"Let us eat these flowers together," said Odysseus kindly.

98
00:05:56.812 --> 00:05:57.852
They happily followed him.

99
00:05:58.313 --> 00:06:02.696
When they got back to the ships, Odysseus ordered the two men tied to their benches and the

100
00:06:02.736 --> 00:06:03.676
flowers destroyed.

101
00:06:04.237 --> 00:06:08.880
Odysseus and his men set sail immediately, and as they departed the two men began to howl and

102
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:13.523
groan in anguish, but Odysseus and his men ignored them because they knew that the flowers had been

103
00:06:13.563 --> 00:06:13.984
bewitched.
